Following security audits, the xFusionCorp Industries security team has rolled out new protocols, including the restriction of direct root SSH login.
Your task is to disable direct SSH root login on all app servers within the Stratos Datacenter.

SOLUTION
To disable direct SSH root login, you need to modify the SSH server configuration on each app server. The key directive is PermitRootLogin, which should be set to no .

📋 Steps to Follow
You will need to perform these steps on all three app servers (stapp01, stapp02, stapp03) .

Connect to the server and switch to root privileges:

bash
ssh tony@stapp01
sudo su -
(Replace tony with the appropriate user for each server) .

Edit the SSH configuration file:

bash
vi /etc/ssh/sshd_config
.

Find and change the PermitRootLogin line:
Locate the line that says PermitRootLogin yes (it may be commented out with a #). Change it to:

bash
PermitRootLogin no
Make sure the line is uncommented (no # at the beginning) .

Restart the SSH service to apply the change:

bash
systemctl restart sshd
This step is crucial; the configuration change will not take effect without a restart .

Verify the change (optional):

bash
sshd -T | grep permitrootlogin
The output should show permitrootlogin no .

⚠️ Important Note
Before making this change, ensure you have a non-root user with sudo access and can successfully log in with that user. Disabling root login without an alternative administrative account can lock you out of the server .
